# MVP priority

Implement in this order.

## P0 — absolutely required

* native macOS application
* Shelf data model
* floating Shelf panel
* menu bar item
* global shortcut
* multiple Shelves
* drag files into Shelf
* drag images into Shelf
* drag text into Shelf
* drag URLs into Shelf
* drag items back out
* persistent local storage
* delete/reorder/move items
* visually excellent item cards
* search
* RevenueCat integration
* polished onboarding

## P1 — strong competition features

* local OCR
* Quick Look
* clipboard capture
* multi-select
* grouped drag-out
* colour detection
* code/text detection
* excellent animations and haptics
* configurable keyboard shortcuts

## P2 — only after core UX is excellent

* automatic cleanup/archive suggestions
* duplicate detection
* richer URL previews
* additional metadata
* iCloud sync
* iPhone/iPad companion
* more advanced smart organisation

Do not implement P2 features at the expense of P0/P1 polish.

---

# Success criterion

Shelf succeeds when this sequence feels easier than any existing workflow:

1. User finds something.
2. User throws it onto Shelf.
3. User forgets about organisation.
4. User needs it again.
5. User summons Shelf.
6. User drags it exactly where it needs to go.

The experience should communicate:

**Finder is where you keep things.
Clipboard is what you're using right now.
Shelf is everything in between.**
